@plane z=0 label="Context"
The spec stays `specs/util/util.spec.md`.

Provides small shared utility functions used by validators, fix suggestions, and rule compilation. The module keeps low-level helpers centralized so parsing and validation modules do not duplicate edit-distance or regex-safety logic.

This plane is context. Later planes are lessons, in the order they were learned.
`context.md` stays beside this file.

@plane z=1 label="Key Decisions"
- **Centralized helpers**: Edit-distance and safe-regex helpers live in `util` to avoid duplicate implementations in parser and validator code.
- **Fail closed regex compilation**: User-provided regex patterns return `None` when invalid or too large, keeping callers on the safe path without panics.

@plane z=2 label="Files to Read First"
- `src/util.rs` — Shared helper functions and unit tests.
- `src/validator.rs` — Main caller for regex-safe config patterns and near-miss suggestions.

@plane z=3 label="Current Status"
Stable. The helpers are small, covered by unit tests, and intentionally have no project-internal dependencies.

@plane z=4 label="Notes"
- Keep this module dependency-light. It is imported by validation code that runs on every check.

@plane z=5 label="Lesson: stay inside the root by construction, not by checking (GHSA-7q5v-8jcc-rx54 sweep)"
- verified. A private sweep after the hash-cache advisory found the same bug in a dozen writers: `root.join(...)` then `fs::write`, which follows any link the repository committed. Guarding each write by hand had already drifted: the pre-commit hook and `.gitignore` refused links, the files beside them did not, and several `exists()` guards were passed by a dangling link.
- The project file functions do every read and write through a `cap_std` directory handle on the project root. It resolves each component beneath the handle and cannot be led outside it, so there is no check for a later write to disagree with. The look that comes first only produces the message.
- A link that stays inside the project is followed, because `CLAUDE.md` as a link to `AGENTS.md` is a real setup. The two cases the handle does not settle are handled on purpose: an existing file is opened without the create flag, and a missing one is created exclusively, so a dangling link is never created through.
- Limits: an absolute link target is refused even when it points inside the project. The caches (`hash_cache`, the remote spec cache) keep a stricter rule, no link at all. Source: bug 2026-11-19-install-scaffold-import-and-migrate-commands-write-through. New writers of project files should start here.

@plane z=6 label="Lesson: a read needs a type check and a bound, not only a root (GHSA-7q5v-8jcc-rx54, third change)"
- verified. After the writes were confined, a tester found the reads: `.specsync/sdd.json` was read with `read_to_string`, so a committed link to `/dev/zero` grew to 227 GB on macOS and was OOM-killed on Linux, and a FIFO there made `change status` wait forever. Chough runs that command on every `next` and `status`.
- Confinement alone does not stop either: a FIFO or a device inside the project is still inside the project. Every read now opens without blocking, checks the type on the open handle, and reads at most one byte past a bound the caller names: 4 MiB for state and change records, 8 MiB for specs and source, 64 MiB for the journal and the hash cache.
- Count read sites with a parser that skips only `#[cfg(test)]` items. Cutting each file at its first test-only helper hid every read in `change/sdd_version.rs`, including the one the tester hit; the real count was 204, not 144.
- Keep the rule enforced, not remembered: `no_project_file_is_read_outside_the_bounded_read` fails on any new direct read unless the line says why the file is not a project file. Source: bug 2026-11-19-specsync-reads-project-files-through-links-waits-on-fifos-and.

@plane z=7 label="Lesson: the repository is the boundary, and the Git directory is inside it (GHSA-7q5v-8jcc-rx54, fifth change)"
- verified. An independent review of the first four changes found the boundary in the wrong place twice. Confining to `--root` broke monorepo subprojects whose links stay inside the clone (`packages/app/CLAUDE.md -> ../../AGENTS.md`). And a link that stays inside the clone can still lead into `.git`: on the branch, `change answer` with a committed `change.md -> ../../../.git/hooks/pre-commit` replaced the hook with text the repository chose, which Git runs at the next commit.
- The handle now sits on the Git work tree's top folder (`git rev-parse --show-toplevel`, once per root, with `GIT_DIR` and `GIT_WORK_TREE` unset). The project functions also refuse the Git directory by name (`.git` in any case, trailing dots and spaces trimmed) and by place: a canonical target under `git rev-parse --absolute-git-dir`, which also catches a Git directory with another name. SpecSync's own `.git` writes (`hooks install`) keep their own paths.
- Inputs that came with the clone are not the person's. A hash cache Git tracks can carry a forged pass, so it is not used. A `specs_dir` of `/` sends a walk over the disk, so configured directories must be relative and inside, and every walk has a budget. A blob in history can be gigabytes, so its size is asked before it is read.
- Keep writes enforced like reads: `no_project_file_is_written_outside_the_confined_helpers` fails on any new direct write, copy, rename, removal or `set_permissions` unless its statement says why the path is not a project file. Scan by statement, not by line, so a chained `OpenOptions` carries one marker. Source: bug 2026-11-19-repository-steered-writes-reads-and-caches.
